SUMMARY:5 KM/3 mile Sunday Afternoon Walk in Canton
DESCRIPTION:Haywood County Walking Meetup Group\nThe start/finish point is
  the parking lot for the Rec Park baseball field (not to be confused with 
 the public-school baseball field next to the football stadium—our meetin
 g place is a little further down the road). The field has been rebuilt sin
 ce Hurricane Helene\, and the parking area is still there. Hopefully\, som
 e of the Clyde Elementary School Cardinals Elite Walking Team and their fa
 milies will be joining us for this event.\n\nWe will start our walk at the
  Canton Recreation Park\, go past Pisgah High School\, up to the public li
 brary and the historic cemetery\, down Academy Street\, and through part o
 f downtown on our way back to Canton Greenway and the start point. The up 
 and down are fairly steep.\n\nDress appropriately for the weather. Conside
 r a hat and sunscreen. Tennis shoes are fine. This is all pavement\, but s
 ome might want walking sticks for the up and down\; I plan to walk with a 
 pair of walking sticks. Bring **plenty of water** and a protein snack. Dog
 s on leash are welcome. Some of the sidewalk along well-traveled roads is 
 narrow and some is not in the best condition\, so strollers and wheelchair
 s are not recommended.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/haywood-county-walking-meetup-group/e
 vents/315170247/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260608T155218Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260608T155218Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Haywood County Walking Group
UID:event_315003613@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260618T044421Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260815T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260815T113000
SUMMARY:Walk the Ethnobotany Trail and More at the NC Arboretum
DESCRIPTION:Haywood County Walking Meetup Group\nMeet inside the Baker Vis
 itor Center which opens at 9:00 a.m. Restrooms are available at the start 
 point. We will start walking as soon as everyone has arrived\, hopefully n
 o later than 9:05.\n\nThe walk is a guided 5 KM trail. There are some mode
 rate hills and stair climbing. A significant part of the route is on well-
 groomed trails with very few obstacles. Some people will want walking pole
 s.\n\nWe will walk down Running Cedar Road to Bent Creak Road. Going down 
 Bent Creek Road\, we will turn onto the Ethnobotany Trail\, and then back 
 onto Bent Creek Road. We will go back up Wesley Branch Trail to the Willow
  Pond and thence back to the start point.\n\nDress for the weather\; consi
 der a hat and sunscreen. Sneakers should be fine\, unless it has rained re
 cently. Be sure to bring **plenty of water** and a protein snack.\n\nDogs 
 on leash are welcome but are not allowed in buildings or in the Bonsai Exh
 ibit. Trail strollers should be ok.\n\nThe NC Arboretum does not have an a
 dmission fee\, but they charge $25 per car parking fee (so pack as many pe
 ople in your car as you can). We recommend the driver purchase the lowest 
 level annual membership which includes free parking. (Of course\, you may 
 want a higher-level membership\, your choice.) We walk at the Arboretum at
  least six times per year.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/haywood-county-walking-meetup-group/e
 vents/315003613/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260528T233934Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260528T233934Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Haywood County Walking Group
UID:event_315092115@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260618T044421Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260829T093000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260829T120000
SUMMARY:Walk 3 Short\, Easy Trails at Panther Creek State Park TN
DESCRIPTION:Haywood County Walking Meetup Group\nPanther Creek State Park 
 is in Tennessee\, just outside of Morristown. **We will meet at the entran
 ce to the park.** The park covers approximately 1435 acres and is located 
 on the shores of Cherokee Lake. The park offers a variety of recreational 
 opportunities including camping\, fishing\, boating\, picnicking\, swimmin
 g\, hiking\, biking\, and horseback riding.\n\nThe park boasts over 30 mil
 es of world-class trails ranging from easy to difficult. Visitors are enco
 uraged to Leave No Trace when using the trails. Hikers can enjoy a magnifi
 cent view of Cherokee Lake and the Cumberland Mountains.\n\nWe will do 2 h
 iking only trails and 1 hiking and biking trail. We will have to travel fr
 om trailhead to trailhead. I've tried to factor that into our time.\n\nThe
  Road to Recycling Trail is a 1.2 mile\, easy\, hiking only trail. This se
 lf-guided trail educates park visitors on the benefits of conserving natur
 al resources. As such\, there are signs along the trail. This trail was aw
 arded the Governor's Greenways and Trails Stewardship Award in 2004.\n\nTh
 e Seven Sinkholes Trail is a 0.6 mile loop\, easy\, hiking only trail. Sin
 kholes develop when rainfall dissolves the under-layer of porous limestone
  rock forming cavities. These cavities and depressions within the limeston
 e gradually enlarge creating sinkholes. The trail meanders around seven di
 stinct sinkholes before dipping into a lush valley of cedar and pine.\n\nO
 ld Wagon Trail is a 1 mile\, easy\, hiking and mountain biking trail. It a
 ppears on the map to be out-and-back 1 mile each way. It is the most popul
 ar trail at the park and is excellent for all ages and abilities. This tra
 il follows the contour of Panther Creek and features a flat\, wide surface
 . This is an excellent trail for the wildlife enthusiast\, as many differe
 nt birds and animals are frequently observed along the trail\, especially 
 in the early morning.\n\nThe park has restrooms in certain areas which we 
 can visit before\, between\, and after the 3 walks. Dress appropriately fo
 r the weather\; consider a hat and sunscreen. I believe tennis shoes are f
 ine\, unless it has rained recently\, in which case you will probably want
  light hiking boots. Most won't want walking sticks\, though I plan to tak
 e just one. As always\, bring plenty of water and a protein snack. I have 
 no information on limitations on dogs (although a park that has horses cou
 ld hardly complain about dog owners who cleans up their pets' messes). Bei
 ng easy trails\, I would think trail strollers would work. Wheelchairs wou
 ld probably work on Old Wagon Trail\, although I'm not 100% certain.\n\nI 
 have no information on an entrance fee to the park. Come prepared just in 
 case. The walk itself is free.\n\nAs you may surmise\, I have not done thi
 s event previously. You are invited to come and try out this new place wit
 h me.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/haywood-county-walking-meetup-group/e
 vents/315092115/
